  "query_preview": "..\n\nClosed low pressure system swirls across the central and eastern \nGreat Lakes today. Cold advection on the back side of this system \nwith the help of the relatively warmer lakes continues to maintain a \nhigh degree of lake clouds and scattered rain showers/drizzle across \nSE MI this afternoon. Iso-Sct rain showers existed across much of SE\nMI earlier this afternoon with surface temperatures in the lower-mid\n40s. The melting layer was and remains a little too elevated above \nthe surface across the forecast area to confidently put any chance of\nsnow into this evening/tonight, so rain p-type will be prevailing in\nthis forecast package. Chance of precipitation will become focused \nmore across the Thumb and east/central portions of the CWA heading \ninto tonight with high degree of cloud cover remaining. Cold \nadvection supports low temperatures tonight down to around freezing. \n\nLow pressure will draw further to the east bringing an end to \nprecipitation chances for most of southeast Michigan tomorrow \nmorning. Though some precipitation chances may hang around across \nfar eastern portions towards the late morning hours. Moist cyclonic \nflow maintains a fair amount of clouds into tomorrow afternoon. A \nshortwave ridge quickly moves over the area tomorrow night bringing \na period of warm advection with W-SW flow. The next Canadian trough \nwill be rather progressive as it swings through behind the ridge. The\ngreatest height falls and lift remain far enough to the north that \nbest associated precipitation chances will hold mainly over Lake \nHuron that potential clip the northern Thumb. As it passes by, \nwesterly winds will become well mixed and offer a gusty winds of 25 \nto 30 mph on Sunday. \n\nThe milder conditions moving into the Great Lakes on Sunday will \ncarry into early next week. Daytime highs into the mid-upper 50s for \nMonday with a slight increase into Tuesday with highs forecast to \neclipse 60 degrees. Passing ridge on Monday will keep conditions dry \nfor the day. Another wave Monday night into early Tuesday may offer \nanother glancing chance at rain across the north, but otherwise \nmainly dry. Long range models depict greater amplitude troughing mid \nto late next week bringing better chances for precipitation and a \nreturn to cooler/near to slightly below average temperatures.",
